// pbgen — the code generator, as a contract rather than as a program.
//
// Every body is a `todo()`.  The signatures are the design, and the
// compiler checks them as claims; see the README for the stamp.
//
// WHAT A GENERATOR IS, HERE.  A pure function from a descriptor to
// novo-lang source text.  It opens nothing, writes nothing and runs no
// compiler: `generate` answers a list of files-as-strings and the host
// decides where they go.  That is what keeps this package `core`, and
// it is also what makes the generator TESTABLE — a golden test is a
// string comparison, not a build.
//
// WHY THE CONTRACT IS PUBLISHED BEFORE THE GENERATOR.  Generated code
// is an API that a hundred other files depend on, and changing it later
// is a migration rather than a patch.  The naming rules, the shape of a
// oneof, the shape of a map, and what proto3 presence turns into are
// decided here, in signatures a reviewer can argue with, before a line
// of the generator exists.  The README's "the generated-code contract"
// section is the same decisions in prose with a worked example beside
// them.
//
// THE NAMING FUNCTIONS ARE THE CONTRACT.  `novo_type_name`,
// `novo_field_name` and the rest are `pub` because they are the part of
// the contract a test can pin: a golden file proves one example, and
// `novo_type_name(".acme.Order") == "AcmeOrder"` proves the rule.  They
// are also what a SECOND generator — a gRPC stub generator, say — has
// to agree with, and grpc-codec-nv's service stubs are exactly that
// second generator.

// What the generator writes, for one output file.
//
// `source` is text and not bytes: a generator that produced bytes would
// be inviting a caller to put something in them that is not source.
pub struct PbGenerated
    // The path relative to the output root: `acme/order.nv`.  Derived
    // from the `.proto` file's own path, so two files that did not
    // collide as `.proto` do not collide as novo-lang.
    path: Str
    // The module name a `use` line writes: `acme_order`.
    module: Str
    // The novo-lang source.  Complete, formatted the way `novo fmt`
    // formats it, and compilable on its own given the modules it
    // imports.
    source: Str

// What the generator may be asked to vary.
//
// Deliberately short.  Every option is a way for two generated trees to
// differ, and the ones that are not here — field ordering, comment
// style, whether to emit builders — are not here because the answer
// should be the same everywhere.
pub struct PbGenOptions
    // Prefixed to every generated module name, for a project that
    // generates into a subdirectory.  `""` for none.
    module_prefix: Str
    // Emit `impl Serialize` and `impl Deserialize` beside the wire
    // codecs, so a generated struct can also travel as JSON or CBOR
    // through the standard library's traits.  On by default; a
    // generated struct with them costs nothing to a caller that never
    // calls them.
    serde_impls: Bool
    // Carry the `.proto` file's comments into the generated source as
    // doc comments.  On by default, because a generated file with no
    // prose is a file nobody reads twice.
    carry_comments: Bool
    // Emit the file's descriptor bytes as a `const`, so a generated
    // module can answer a reflection request about itself without the
    // original `.proto` file.
    embed_descriptor: Bool

// The options every generated tree should start from.
//
// ```novo
// use pbgen
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pbgen.default_options().serde_impls}")   // true
// ```
pub fn default_options() -> PbGenOptions []
    todo("pbgen.default_options")

// Generate novo-lang source for one file.
//
// The signature the grid's row for this package names, and the one a
// caller reaches for.  An EMPTY LIST IS A REFUSAL: the generator does
// not guess at a file it cannot generate correctly, and `refusals` is
// where the reason is.  A caller that wants both at once calls
// `generate_set`.
//
// One file in, one or more files out — one for the messages and enums,
// and one more per service when the file declares any, because a
// program that wants the types should not have to compile the stubs.
pub fn generate(file: PbFile) -> [PbGenerated] []
    todo("pbgen.generate")

// Generate for a whole descriptor set, with names resolved across
// files.
//
// The form a build system uses: a `type_name` that points into an
// imported file resolves here and cannot resolve in `generate`, which
// sees one file and its imports' names but not their contents.
pub fn generate_set(set: PbFileSet, opts: PbGenOptions) -> Result<[PbGenerated], PbSchemaError> []
    todo("pbgen.generate_set")

// Every reason the generator will not generate for this file.
//
// An empty list means `generate` will produce something.  The list is
// what a build tool prints, and the four entries it can hold today are
// named in the README:
//
//   an EXTENSION, which needs a run-time registry the generated code
//   would consult, and that is a design of its own;
//   a GROUP, which is readable on the wire and has no novo-lang shape
//   worth generating — a caller that meets one uses `pbdyn`;
//   an EDITION file, whose feature resolution is a specification this
//   package does not implement;
//   a name that no renaming rule can make unique — two `.proto`
//   messages whose novo-lang names would collide, which the global
//   type namespace makes a refusal rather than a warning.
pub fn refusals(file: PbFile) -> [PbSchemaError] []
    todo("pbgen.refusals")

// ── the naming rules, as functions a test can pin ────────────────────

// The novo-lang type name for a fully-qualified protobuf name.
//
// The package path is folded into the name in upper camel case:
// `.acme.orders.Order` becomes `AcmeOrdersOrder`.  That is uglier than
// `Order` and it is not a preference — novo-lang keys struct identity
// by NAME across the whole assembly, dependencies included, so two
// generated trees that both declared `Order` could not be used by one
// program (`docs/publishing.md` § Public type names are globally
// unique).  The `.proto` file's own package is the disambiguator the
// author already chose, so it is the one to use.
//
// ```novo
// use pbgen
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(pbgen.novo_type_name(".acme.orders.Order"))   // AcmeOrdersOrder
// ```
pub fn novo_type_name(full_name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.novo_type_name")

// The novo-lang field name for a `.proto` field name.
//
// The `.proto` name unchanged — protobuf's convention is already
// snake case and novo-lang's is too — except for a name that is a
// novo-lang reserved word, which takes a trailing underscore.
// `then`, `loop` and `register` are the ones a real `.proto` file
// meets.
//
// ```novo
// use pbgen
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let a = pbgen.novo_field_name("unit_price")
//     let b = pbgen.novo_field_name("loop")
//     println("${a} ${b}")   // unit_price loop_
// ```
pub fn novo_field_name(name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.novo_field_name")

// The generated module name for a `.proto` file path.
//
// `acme/order.proto` becomes `acme_order`: the directories joined with
// underscores, the extension dropped.  A module name is unique across
// the assembly the same way a type name is, so the path is folded in
// for the same reason.
pub fn novo_module_name(file_name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.novo_module_name")

// The generated enum variant name for one value of one enum.
//
// The enum's novo-lang name, then the value's name in upper camel case
// with the enum's own prefix stripped when it has one:
// `.acme.Currency`'s `CURRENCY_EUR` becomes `AcmeCurrencyEur`.  The
// prefix strip is what protobuf's own style guide asks generators to
// do, and the enum name is prepended because a VARIANT is resolved by
// name across the assembly too — `Err(Transport(9))` binds whichever
// `Transport` was declared first.
pub fn novo_variant_name(enum_full_name: Str, value_name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.novo_variant_name")

// The generated enum type name for a oneof.
//
// A oneof becomes an enum of its own, named for its message and
// itself: `.acme.Order`'s `payment` oneof becomes `AcmeOrderPayment`.
// The message's own struct holds it as `?AcmeOrderPayment`, because a
// oneof with nothing set is the ordinary state.
pub fn novo_oneof_type_name(message_full_name: Str, oneof_name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.novo_oneof_type_name")

// The novo-lang type a scalar protobuf type generates as.
//
// `Int` for every integral type, `Float` for `double` and `float`,
// `Bool` for `bool`, `Str` for `string`, `Bytes` for `bytes`.  Eight
// integral types onto one `Int` is the honest mapping — novo-lang has
// one integer type and it is sixty-four bits signed — and what it costs
// is named in the README's limits: `uint64` above 2^63 is the same bits
// read as a negative, and a generated round trip preserves it.
//
// ```novo
// use pbgen
// use pbwire
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(pbgen.novo_scalar_type(PbTypeSfixed32))   // Int
// ```
pub fn novo_scalar_type(t: PbFieldType) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.novo_scalar_type")

// The whole novo-lang type a field generates as, presence and
// repetition included.
//
// `[Int]` for a repeated `int32`; `?Str` for a `string` with presence;
// `Str` for a proto3 singular `string` without it;
// `[AcmeOrderTagsEntry]` for a `map<string, int32>` — a list of pairs,
// which is the shape the README's contract section explains.
pub fn novo_field_type(set: PbFileSet, f: PbField, syntax: PbSyntax) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.novo_field_type")

// The novo-lang type name for a map field's pair struct.
//
// `.acme.Order`'s `tags` map becomes `AcmeOrderTagsEntry`, with fields
// `key` and `value`.
pub fn novo_map_entry_type_name(message_full_name: Str, field_name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.novo_map_entry_type_name")

// Whether a word is one novo-lang reserves, so a generated identifier
// must not be it.
//
// `then`, `loop` and `register` among them.  Published because the
// generator is not the only thing that needs the list — a hand-written
// wrapper around generated code needs the same answer.
pub fn is_reserved_word(s: Str) -> Bool []
    todo("pbgen.is_reserved_word")

// ── what the generated code looks like, as facts ─────────────────────

// The name of the generated function that encodes a message:
// `encode_acme_order`.
//
// A free function rather than a method, because `impl` blocks on a
// generated struct would put two of this package's decisions — the
// trait and the method name — where a caller's own `impl` might want
// to be.
pub fn encode_fn_name(message_full_name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.encode_fn_name")

// The name of the generated function that decodes one:
// `decode_acme_order`.
pub fn decode_fn_name(message_full_name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.decode_fn_name")

// The name of the generated function that sizes one:
// `encoded_len_acme_order`.
//
// Generated because the sizing pass is where the length prefix problem
// is solved (`pbwrite` § the length prefix is the whole problem), and a
// caller that wants to write into its own buffer needs the number
// first.
pub fn len_fn_name(message_full_name: Str) -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.len_fn_name")

// The name of the generated field that holds a message's unknown
// fields: `unknown`.
//
// Every generated struct has one, and the generated encoder writes it
// last.  A struct without it would be a struct that silently drops a
// newer peer's fields, which is the failure this whole package is
// arranged to prevent.
pub fn unknown_field_name() -> Str []
    todo("pbgen.unknown_field_name")
